using namespace std;

// Function prototype
string convertMarkdownToHTML(const string& markdown);

int main() {
    // Example usage
    const string input = "# Hello world\n\nThis is some **markdown**";

    string output = convertMarkdownToHTML(input);
    cout << "Input:\n" << input << "\nOutput as HTML:\n" << output << endl;

    return 0;
}

// Function definition
string convertMarkdownToHTML(const string& markdown) {
    const string substitutions[] = { 
        {"# ", "<h1>"},
        {"## ", "<h2>"},
        {"### ", "<h3>"},
        {"#### ", "<h4>"},
        {"##### ", "<h5>"},
        {"###### ", "<h6>"},

        {"\\\\", "\\"},
        {"\\[ *(.+?) *]\\((.*?)\\)", "<img src=\"\\2\" alt=\"\\1\">"}, // Image tag
        {"\\*([^ *]+)\*", "<strong>\\1</strong>"},
        {"_([^ ]+)_", "<em>\\1</em>"},
        {">>(.+? )", "<blockquote>\\1</blockquote><br>"},

        {"---", "<hr style='width:100%;height:2px;border-width:0;color:black;'>"},
        {"___", "<hr style='width:100%;height:2px;border-width:0;color:black;'>"},
    };

    for (auto pair : substitutions) {
        boost::replace_all(markdown, pair.first, pair.second);
    }

    // Generate HTML header/footer
    stringstream ss;
    ss << "<html>\n<head><title>Converted Markdown</title></head>\n<body>";
    ss << markdown;
    ss << "</body></html>";

    return ss.str();
}
```

In this implementation, we define the `convertMarkdownToHTML` function that takes a constant reference to a `std::string` containing markdown-formatted text as input.

We use an array of pairs to map specific markdown elements to their corresponding HTML tags or other formatting adjustments. The pairs consist of two strings; the first being the search pattern in markdown (e.g., `# `, `\\*`, `\`, etc.) and the second being the replacement pattern in HTML markup accordingly (e.g., `<h1>`, `<strong>`, etc.).

After defining our substitution mappings through the `substitutions` array, we loop through each mapping and use Boost's `replace_all` algorithm to replace occurrences of the search patterns with the respective replacements within the input markdown string.

Finally, we generate the necessary HTML headers and footers around the transformed content using an `stringstream`. This allows us to create the overall HTML structure while keeping the original markdown formatting intact.

Note that there may be additional markdown features we haven't covered here, so you'll need to extend or adjust this code based on your specific requirements. Also, we assume that Boost library functions can be used. If it's not included, make sure to include the required header file (`<boost/algorithm/string.hpp>` in this case).

