In Java, `==` is used for comparing references (memory addresses) between objects, whereas `.equals()` method is used for checking the content equality between two objects.

In case of comparing string values, you should use `.equals()` method rather than `==`. This is because strings in Java are immutable, which means they cannot be modified once created. The `==` operator will only check whether both variables refer to the same String object in memory, while `.equals()` checks if their contents (chars) match.

For example:

```
String str1 = "Hello";
String str2 = "World";

System.out.println(str1 == str2); // Outputs: false
System.out.println(str1.equals(str2)); // Outputs: false

String str3 = "Hello";
String str4 = new String("Hello");

System.out.println(str3 == str4); // Outputs: false (different memory locations)
System.out.println(str3.equals(str4)); // Outputs: true (content is equal)
```

So always use `.equals()` method when comparing string values in Java. However, there are some cases where `==` could work as expected, such as when compared with null or when creating multiple references to the same literal string.

```java
// Comparing with null - Works fine with ==
String str1 = null;
String str2 = "";

if(str1 == null) {
    System.out.println("str1 is null");
}

// Creating multiple references to the same literal string - Also works fine with double-equals
String str3 = "Java";
String str4 = "Java";

if(str3 == str4) {
    System.out.println("str3 and str4 have the same value");
}
``` 

In summary, `==` can be used to compare primitive data types, but when dealing with mutable objects like Strings, `.equals()` method is recommended.

